   "read_md": "# A01 \u00b7 Values, types, variables, f-strings\n\n**Why it exists.** Every program is values moving through names. Get these four things straight and the next eleven chapters are just combinations of them.\n\n## The idea\n\nA **value** is a piece of data. Every value has a **type**, which decides what you can do with it.\n\n```python\n42          # int    whole number\n3.5         # float  decimal number\n\"hi\"        # str    text, in quotes\nTrue        # bool   True or False, capital letter\n```\n\nA **variable** is a name stuck to a value. `=` means \"stick this name on this value\", not \"equals\".\n\n```python\nprice = 3.5\nprice = price * 2     # the name now points at 7.0\n```\n\nAsk a value its type with `type(x)`. Convert with `int(\"42\")`, `float(\"3.5\")`, `str(42)`.\n\nAn **f-string** builds text from values. Put `f` before the quote and `{}` around anything you want inserted. A colon inside the braces formats it.\n\n```python\nname, total = \"Zach\", 7.5\nprint(f\"{name} owes ${total:.2f}\")      # Zach owes $7.50\nprint(f\"{3:02d}:{7:02d}\")                # 03:07\n```\n\n`:.2f` means \"float, 2 decimals\". `:02d` means \"integer, pad to width 2 with zeros\".\n\nInteger tricks you'll use constantly: `7 // 2` is `3` (floor division, drops the remainder). `7 % 2` is `1` (the remainder). `2 ** 10` is `1024`.\n\n## The two things people get wrong\n\n1. `\"5\" + 3` crashes. A str and an int don't add. Convert first: `int(\"5\") + 3`.\n2. `=` assigns, `==` compares. `if x = 5` is a syntax error. `if x == 5` is a question.\n\nRun the examples. Change a number. Break something on purpose and read the error.\n",
   "challenge_md": "# A01 challenge \u00b7 Values, types, variables, f-strings\n\nThree levels in `challenge.py`. Each adds one constraint. Pass level 1 before reading level 2.\n\n## Level 1 \u00b7 `kind(value)`\nReturn the type name of a value as a string: `\"int\"`, `\"float\"`, `\"str\"`, or `\"bool\"`.\n`kind(3)` is `\"int\"`. `kind(True)` is `\"bool\"`.\n\n## Level 2 \u00b7 `receipt_line(item, price, qty)`\nReturn one line of a receipt, exactly like `\"3 x Apple @ $0.50 = $1.50\"`. Price and total always show two decimals.\n\n## Level 3 \u00b7 `clock(seconds)`\nTurn a number of seconds into `\"h:mm:ss\"`. `clock(3669)` is `\"1:01:09\"`. Minutes and seconds are always two digits, hours are not padded. Use `//` and `%`, no loops, no `if`.\n\n## Hints (one at a time, after 5 real minutes)\n\n<details>\n<summary>Hint 1 \u00b7 a nudge</summary>\n\nLevel 1: `type(x)` gives you a type object. Type objects have a `.__name__`. Level 2: everything you need is in the f-string section of read.md. Level 3: how many seconds in an hour? In a minute? `//` tells you how many fit, `%` tells you what's left over.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Hint 2 \u00b7 the idea</summary>\n\nLevel 3: `hours = seconds // 3600`. What's left after removing the hours is `seconds % 3600`. Do the same split again for minutes. Format with `:02d`.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Hint 3 \u00b7 the skeleton</summary>\n\n```\nh = seconds // 3600\nrest = seconds % 3600\nm = rest // ____\ns = rest % ____\nreturn f\"{h}:{m:02d}:{s:02d}\"\n```\n\n</details>\n",
